"_____________________________________________________________"
"Pattern Matching Guards and Patterns in Bern"
"A clause matches only when its patterns match AND its when-guard is true."
"_____________________________________________________________\n"

"[1. Guards on function clauses]"
"Add 'when <condition>' after the parameters. Clauses are tried top to bottom."
"def classify(n) when n > 0 -> \"positive\""
"def classify(0) -> \"zero\""
"def classify(n) -> \"negative\""

"\nResult:"
def classify(n) when n > 0 -> "positive"
def classify(0) -> "zero"
def classify(n) -> "negative"
classify(7)
classify(0)
classify(-3)

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[2. Guards with block bodies]"
"Guards work with do ... end bodies too."
"def grade(n) when n >= 90 do return \"A\" end"
"def grade(n) when n >= 80 -> \"B\""
"def grade(_) -> \"C\""

"\nResult:"
def grade(n) when n >= 90 do
    return "A"
end
def grade(n) when n >= 80 -> "B"
def grade(_) -> "C"
grade(95)
grade(85)
grade(50)

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[3. Guards in case expressions]"
"Each branch may carry its own when-guard before the = sign."
"case n is x when x > 100 = \"big\" | x when x > 10 = \"medium\" | _ = \"small\" end"

"\nResult:"
def size(n) -> case n is x when x > 100 = "big" | x when x > 10 = "medium" | _ = "small" end
size(500)
size(50)
size(5)

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[4. Guards on lambdas]"
"Lambdas accept a when-guard before the arrow."
"check_pos = \\x when x > 0 -> \"yes\""

"\nResult:"
check_pos = \x when x > 0 -> "yes"
check_pos(4)

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[5. Negative literal patterns]"
"Negative numbers can be matched as literals."
"def sign(-1) -> \"minus one\""
"def sign(_) -> \"other\""

"\nResult:"
def sign(-1) -> "minus one"
def sign(_) -> "other"
sign(-1)
sign(8)

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[6. Repeated variables must be equal]"
"If a variable appears twice in a pattern, the values must match."
"def same(x, x) -> \"equal\""
"def same(_, _) -> \"different\""

"\nResult:"
def same(x, x) -> "equal"
def same(_, _) -> "different"
same(3, 3)
same(3, 4)
